WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.310 --> 00:00:04.160
When we think of the richest families in the
world, we think of people like the Walton

00:00:04.160 --> 00:00:06.509
family and the Saudi Royal family.

00:00:06.509 --> 00:00:11.171
And though they do have enormous amounts of
wealth, they’re not exactly 100% family

00:00:11.171 --> 00:00:12.980
businesses today.

00:00:12.980 --> 00:00:17.259
Walmart is a public company and half the company
is owned by investors.

00:00:17.259 --> 00:00:22.220
Meanwhile, the Saudi Royal family is more
of a dictatorship than a family business.

00:00:22.220 --> 00:00:26.220
There is one hundred billion dollar conglomerate
though that is still almost completely owned

00:00:26.220 --> 00:00:30.199
by the founding family and that is of course
the infamous Koch brothers.

00:00:30.199 --> 00:00:34.570
Today, the richest two Koch heirs themselves
are worth $122.6 billion.

00:00:34.570 --> 00:00:40.240
So, here’s how the Koch brothers ended up
owning the world’s richest private family

00:00:40.240 --> 00:00:42.280
business.

00:00:42.280 --> 00:00:47.270
It all started on September 23, 1900, when
Fred Chase Koch was born in Quanah Texas.

00:00:47.270 --> 00:00:51.990
Fred’s father Harry Koch was a Dutch immigrant
who originally worked as a printer’s apprentice

00:00:51.990 --> 00:00:53.530
back in the Netherlands.

00:00:53.530 --> 00:00:58.350
Harry had immigrated to the US with the American
dream and he bought the Tribune-Chief Newspaper

00:00:58.350 --> 00:01:00.460
after he arrived in America.

00:01:00.460 --> 00:01:04.650
This provided the family a decent living,
but nothing too crazy.

00:01:04.650 --> 00:01:09.270
Fred would end up attending Rice University
in 1917 where he spent two years before transferring

00:01:09.270 --> 00:01:10.740
to MIT.

00:01:10.740 --> 00:01:15.049
Fred finished up his chemical engineering
degree at MIT in 1922 and instantly jumped

00:01:15.049 --> 00:01:16.560
into the workforce.

00:01:16.560 --> 00:01:20.200
Fred scored a job at Texaco probably as a
chemical engineer.

00:01:20.200 --> 00:01:24.520
He jumped around from job to job, working
up the corporate ladder with each switch.

00:01:24.520 --> 00:01:29.090
Within a few years, Fred had secured a job
as chief engineer at the Medway Oil &amp; Storage

00:01:29.090 --> 00:01:30.090
Company.

00:01:30.090 --> 00:01:34.170
Though this was likely a super nice gig, Fred
wasn’t quite satisfied.

00:01:34.170 --> 00:01:37.719
He wanted to follow in his father’s footsteps
and start his own company.

00:01:37.719 --> 00:01:43.020
So, in 1925, he reached out to a classmate
from MIT named Keith who had started his own

00:01:43.020 --> 00:01:45.509
company called Keith Winkler Engineering Company.

00:01:45.509 --> 00:01:49.859
Keith eventually quit the business and the
company would be left to Fred.

00:01:49.859 --> 00:01:53.789
Being an extremely small player within the
oil industry, Fred had to think of some creative

00:01:53.789 --> 00:01:56.189
solutions to efficiently compete against the
big players.

00:01:56.189 --> 00:02:00.359
And Fred’s first step in this direction
was developing a more efficient thermal cracking

00:02:00.359 --> 00:02:05.039
process which allowed small refineries to
turn crude oil into gasoline.

00:02:05.039 --> 00:02:08.979
Larger oil companies quickly caught sight
of this invention and decided to crush Fred

00:02:08.979 --> 00:02:09.979
with litigation.

00:02:09.979 --> 00:02:15.500
They launched 44 lawsuits against Fred which
of course drowned him for many years.

00:02:15.500 --> 00:02:19.150
Given that most of these lawsuits were utter
BS designed to simply push out Fred from the

00:02:19.150 --> 00:02:22.950
business, Fred ended up winning basically
every single lawsuit.

00:02:22.950 --> 00:02:28.000
Fred won 43 out of the 44 lawsuits filed against
him, and it was eventually discovered that

00:02:28.000 --> 00:02:30.640
the lawsuit he lost was due to a bribed judge.

00:02:30.640 --> 00:02:33.630
So really, he won every single one of them.

00:02:33.630 --> 00:02:37.730
Anyway, as Fred dealt with the litigation,
it was extremely difficult for him to conduct

00:02:37.730 --> 00:02:39.209
business in the US.

00:02:39.209 --> 00:02:42.430
So, he took his invention to the Soviet Union.

00:02:42.430 --> 00:02:46.520
At first, Fred was extremely cautious when
it came to the Soviets as he was worried that

00:02:46.520 --> 00:02:48.000
they wouldn’t pay him.

00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:52.150
So, Fred demanded that he be paid upfront
and the Soviets agreed.

00:02:52.150 --> 00:02:56.870
In 1929, Fred started working with Soviet
officials to set up 15 oil refineries in the

00:02:56.870 --> 00:02:57.870
Soviet Union.

00:02:57.870 --> 00:03:00.849
He also trained Bolshevik engineers to operate
and run these refineries.

00:03:00.849 --> 00:03:04.580
For many years, it seemed like the partnership
was progressing phenomenally.

00:03:04.580 --> 00:03:07.560
Fred had earned about $500,000.

00:03:07.560 --> 00:03:11.530
But once the Soviets got a hang of things,
they decided to cut out Fred.

00:03:11.530 --> 00:03:16.409
Stalin started taking out Koch employees which
quickly pushed Fred out of the Soviet Union,

00:03:16.409 --> 00:03:18.920
regretting the collaboration altogether.

00:03:18.920 --> 00:03:23.430
Back at home, Fred was still handling the
onslaught of lawsuits, so he looked for other

00:03:23.430 --> 00:03:26.560
international opportunities and he would land
on Germany.

00:03:26.560 --> 00:03:31.629
Germany was being led by some crazy people
with crazy beliefs, but there was no denying

00:03:31.629 --> 00:03:35.610
that the country was progressing rapidly,
so Fred took on the opportunity.

00:03:35.610 --> 00:03:39.549
Fred ended up building a massive refinery
in Hamburg which was personally approved by

00:03:39.549 --> 00:03:41.040
Hitler himself.

00:03:41.040 --> 00:03:46.140
The Koch family has since tried to cover up
this part of the story for obvious reasons,

00:03:46.140 --> 00:03:49.840
but the high frequency of Fred’s visits
to Germany suggests that Germany was indeed

00:03:49.840 --> 00:03:52.810
a major part of his business during the 1930s.

00:03:52.810 --> 00:03:56.540
Fun fact, rumors suggest that Fred almost
boarded the Hindenburg during one of his trips

00:03:56.540 --> 00:03:57.540
to Germany.

00:03:57.540 --> 00:04:00.659
But, a scheduling delay saved him from the
accident.

00:04:00.659 --> 00:04:04.840
By the end of the 1930s, Fred finished up
with his commitments to Germany and things

00:04:04.840 --> 00:04:07.319
were looking better than ever before.

00:04:07.319 --> 00:04:11.760
Fred had become quite wealthy from his business
in the Soviet Union and Germany and he was

00:04:11.760 --> 00:04:14.069
able to form international connections.

00:04:14.069 --> 00:04:19.430
In the meantime, the 44 lawsuits were finally
winding down after 15 years, and they actually

00:04:19.430 --> 00:04:25.020
turned out to be a good thing as Fred ended
up winning $1.5 million through the lawsuits.

00:04:25.020 --> 00:04:28.640
Ironically, that’s three times the amount
he was paid by Stalin.

00:04:28.640 --> 00:04:33.330
With all of this money, connections, and experience,
Fred was finally able to turn his attention

00:04:33.330 --> 00:04:38.250
onto the American market in 1941 with the
founding of Koch Engineering Company.

00:04:38.250 --> 00:04:41.370
The next 20 years were pretty much smooth
sailing for Fred.

00:04:41.370 --> 00:04:46.280
Having worked with Stalin and Hitler and defended
himself from the oil giants, actually growing

00:04:46.280 --> 00:04:49.919
an oil company in America wasn’t that difficult
for Fred.

00:04:49.919 --> 00:04:55.320
He made a plethora of partnerships, acquisitions,
and deals, and soon enough Fred become a multi

00:04:55.320 --> 00:04:58.380
decamillionaire businessman by the 1960s.

00:04:58.380 --> 00:05:02.250
And this is when he would face his last major
challenge which was figuring out what to do

00:05:02.250 --> 00:05:04.539
with his business after his death.

00:05:04.539 --> 00:05:08.470
Fred had four sons and was hoping that as
they grew up, they would develop an interest

00:05:08.470 --> 00:05:09.750
in the family business.

00:05:09.750 --> 00:05:14.330
But this wasn’t the case, while his children
did care about preserving the family wealth,

00:05:14.330 --> 00:05:17.789
none of his children really cared about running
the business itself.

00:05:17.789 --> 00:05:22.991
This was especially true with his oldest son
Fred Koch Jr. Fred Jr. attended Harvard, but

00:05:22.991 --> 00:05:27.220
he got a humanities degree which was not very
applicable to the family business.

00:05:27.220 --> 00:05:32.750
Moreover, Fred Jr. joined the US Navy after
graduation, so Fred Jr. wasn’t really a

00:05:32.750 --> 00:05:33.750
choice.

00:05:33.750 --> 00:05:36.949
As a result, Fred would end up choosing his
most educated son Charles Koch.

00:05:36.949 --> 00:05:41.120
Charles had also attended MIT like Fred, and
he had a bachelor’s in general engineering

00:05:41.120 --> 00:05:44.260
and a dual master’s in nuclear and chemical
engineering.

00:05:44.260 --> 00:05:48.080
Despite being well qualified to run the business,
Charles also wasn’t interested in taking

00:05:48.080 --> 00:05:49.770
over the family business.

00:05:49.770 --> 00:05:53.460
It wasn’t until Fred made an ultimatum that
he was going to sell the company if Charles

00:05:53.460 --> 00:05:56.229
didn’t take over that Charles joined the
company.

00:05:56.229 --> 00:06:00.580
Charles joined the company in 1961, and it
was pretty good timing as Fred passed away

00:06:00.580 --> 00:06:04.000
just 6 years later in 1967 due to a heart
attack.

00:06:04.000 --> 00:06:07.950
Charles would of course the new CEO and chairman
of the company.

00:06:07.950 --> 00:06:11.960
A couple of years later, Charles’s brother,
David, would also join the company as a technical

00:06:11.960 --> 00:06:13.430
services manager.

00:06:13.430 --> 00:06:18.080
David also had a bachelor’s and master’s
in chemical engineering from MIT, so he fit

00:06:18.080 --> 00:06:19.129
right in.

00:06:19.129 --> 00:06:23.000
David would eventually end up opening a new
office in New York and become the president

00:06:23.000 --> 00:06:25.000
of one of the company’s divisions.

00:06:25.000 --> 00:06:28.569
At this point, Charles had done most of the
heavy lifting when it came to actually running

00:06:28.569 --> 00:06:31.120
and growing the business after his father’s
death.

00:06:31.120 --> 00:06:33.520
And David was the second most involved.

00:06:33.520 --> 00:06:38.780
Bill didn’t join the company until 1974,
but he would stir up the pot quite heavily.

00:06:38.780 --> 00:06:43.440
Bill had also gone to MIT and not only did
he have a bachelor’s and master’s, but

00:06:43.440 --> 00:06:45.699
also a doctoral degree in chemical engineering.

00:06:45.699 --> 00:06:50.770
So, you would think that he would fit right
in, but this wasn’t the case.

00:06:50.770 --> 00:06:54.900
According to Charles, Bill wanted higher-level
positions, but the divisions that Bill ran

00:06:54.900 --> 00:06:56.789
were usually not as profitable.

00:06:56.789 --> 00:07:00.680
So, it didn’t make sense to promote him
up the corporate ladder.

00:07:00.680 --> 00:07:04.620
Bill also had a massive problem with how Charles
was spending the company’s money.

00:07:04.620 --> 00:07:10.379
Charles was and still is a super capitalist
and despises socialistic ideas.

00:07:10.379 --> 00:07:14.620
His father had gotten screwed over by Stalin,
so Charles felt that nothing good could come

00:07:14.620 --> 00:07:19.129
out of socialistic programs like social security,
minimum wage requirements, and welfare.

00:07:19.129 --> 00:07:22.639
And, Charles had been spending large amounts
of money funding libertarian presidential

00:07:22.639 --> 00:07:25.030
candidates who upheld these beliefs.

00:07:25.030 --> 00:07:30.050
Bill hated this and these tensions would blow
over in Christmas of 1979 when Bill ignited

00:07:30.050 --> 00:07:32.270
a flame at the family get-together.

00:07:32.270 --> 00:07:35.879
Bill suggested that just because Charles had
grown the company doesn’t mean that the

00:07:35.879 --> 00:07:39.610
other brothers don’t have a right to the
decide future of the company.

00:07:39.610 --> 00:07:43.700
At this point, their mother was still alive
and she was technically the one who owned

00:07:43.700 --> 00:07:44.700
the company.

00:07:44.700 --> 00:07:48.270
So, Bill asked their mother how she was going
to split up the company.

00:07:48.270 --> 00:07:52.449
Their mother started crying that it had come
down to this and left the dinner table, and

00:07:52.449 --> 00:07:55.520
this was the last Christmas the Kochs spent
together.

00:07:55.520 --> 00:07:59.419
Bill went ahead and got some other board members
on his side along with their other brother

00:07:59.419 --> 00:08:00.419
Fred Jr.

00:08:00.419 --> 00:08:04.379
Ironically, the two brothers who had done
the least for the company were trying to take

00:08:04.379 --> 00:08:05.819
over the company.

00:08:05.819 --> 00:08:10.169
Bill organized a coup to fire Charles and
takeover, but fortunately for Charles, he

00:08:10.169 --> 00:08:12.710
would have just barely enough votes to keep
him in power.

00:08:12.710 --> 00:08:18.050
Though Bill’s coup had failed, it was clear
how big of a threat he was, so Charles decided

00:08:18.050 --> 00:08:20.630
to give Bill what he wanted and move on.

00:08:20.630 --> 00:08:24.610
Both Bill and Charles called in a bunch of
financial institutions like Morgan Stanley

00:08:24.610 --> 00:08:26.580
and Goldman Sachs to value Koch Industries.

00:08:26.580 --> 00:08:32.810
On June 4, 1983, Charles arranged for a buyout
of Bill and Fred Jr’s stakes in the business.

00:08:32.810 --> 00:08:37.539
Bill was given $470 million and Fred Jr. was
given $330 million.

00:08:37.539 --> 00:08:41.700
Charles hoped that this would finally bring
peace to Koch industries, but the peace was

00:08:41.700 --> 00:08:42.899
short-lived.

00:08:42.899 --> 00:08:46.590
Just a few years later, Bill started to complain
that he was underpaid for his stake in the

00:08:46.590 --> 00:08:50.500
business and starting filing a bunch of lawsuits
against Koch Industries.

00:08:50.500 --> 00:08:55.390
Bill pursued these lawsuits up until the late
1990s and he did get a little bit more money,

00:08:55.390 --> 00:08:57.970
but eventually, Bill did give in.

00:08:57.970 --> 00:09:01.720
Bill walked away would a great amount of money,
but he destroyed his relationship with Charles

00:09:01.720 --> 00:09:05.899
and David and just sticking around would’ve
resulted in way more money.

00:09:05.899 --> 00:09:08.560
But this is the path Bill chose.

00:09:08.560 --> 00:09:13.760
In the meantime, Charles owned 42% of the
company and David owned another 42%.

00:09:13.760 --> 00:09:17.910
The final 16% was owned by one of their father’s
friends Howard Marshall II.

00:09:17.910 --> 00:09:24.340
Today, Koch industries has roughly 100,000
employees and pulls in $115 billion in revenue

00:09:24.340 --> 00:09:25.340
annually.

00:09:25.340 --> 00:09:28.660
To put that into perspective, that’s $30
billion more than Facebook.

00:09:28.660 --> 00:09:33.550
So, it’s not surprising that either David
and Charles’s fortunes are each worth $62

00:09:33.550 --> 00:09:34.550
billion.

00:09:34.550 --> 00:09:38.779
Unfortunately, David died about two years
ago due to prostate cancer, and his wealth

00:09:38.779 --> 00:09:40.870
is now managed by his wife Julia.

00:09:40.870 --> 00:09:45.670
Charles on the other hand is still alive at
85 years old, and he is still the CEO and

00:09:45.670 --> 00:09:46.670
chairman of Koch industries.

00:09:46.670 --> 00:09:47.670
He’s literally been CEO for 54 years.

00:09:47.670 --> 00:09:48.670
It doesn’t look like Charles and Bill ever
made amends and they’ve been living completely

00:09:48.670 --> 00:09:49.670
separate lives since 1979.

00:09:49.670 --> 00:09:50.670
And that’s the story of the world’s richest
family business.

00:09:50.670 --> 00:09:53.000
Do you guys think Bill’s fight was justified?

00:09:53.000 --> 00:09:54.000
